lz4_opts fixtures (src/lz4_opts/tests.rs)

ref.txt      SHA-256 digests of the reference tools' output, one row per
             input x source x flags; its header says what each row means.
             The C tier (lz4-c) must reproduce every row byte for byte.

*.lz4        The reference lz4's own output, read by both tiers:

  small.lz4             lz4 -q -c small
  small-cs-bx.lz4       lz4 -q -c --content-size -BX small
  small-nocrc.lz4       lz4 -q -c --no-frame-crc small
  small-d10.lz4         lz4 -q -c -D d10 small
  small-legacy.lz4      lz4 -q -c -l small
  multi-b4-bd.lz4       lz4 -q -c -B4 -BD multi
  multi-b4-bd-d10.lz4   lz4 -q -c -B4 -BD -D d10 multi
  multi-b4-bx-9-cs.lz4  lz4 -q -c -B4 -BX -9 --content-size multi
  rand-b4-bd.lz4        lz4 -q -c -B4 -BD rand66
  empty.lz4             lz4 -q -c empty
  rep9-bd.lz4           lz4 -q -c -BD rep9      (57,904 bytes)
  rep9-legacy.lz4       lz4 -q -c -l rep9       (78,293 bytes)

  The inputs and the dictionary are generated, not stored: small, multi,
  rand66, empty and rep9 (9 MiB + 37 of a 50,000-byte text repeated, so
  three 4 MiB jobs and two 8 MiB legacy blocks) are tests.rs' fixture_input(), d10 its dictionary().

Reference tools: lz4 1.10.0 (/opt/homebrew/opt/lz4/bin/lz4, or
$STUFFR_LZ4_REFERENCE) and zstd 1.5.7 (/opt/homebrew/opt/zstd/bin/zstd, or
$STUFFR_ZSTD_REFERENCE), on macOS. Regenerate everything with

  STUFFR_LZ4_OPTS_REGEN=<dir> cargo test -p stuffr-formats --features lz4 \
      --lib lz4_opts::tests::regenerate_fixtures -- --ignored

then copy <dir>/* here.
